# The tsum portrait library. GENERATED -- do not edit by hand.
#
# Written by the development tools' lexicon (`name_handling/build_library.py`
# there), which reconstructs each pre-round button from the game's own art
# rather than from a screenshot: the tsum's sprite composited over the game's
# blank button, at a transform fitted against real captures. So a signature
# here and the icon on screen are the same picture, and the only differences a
# live capture can have are the three things the game draws over the icon --
# the store ribbon, the Lv badge, and a hint toast.
#
# One tsum per line, tab separated: the game's own id for it -- the key its
# sprite and its printed name share, and what the CSV records -- then the name
# the global build prints, then the name the Japanese build prints, then the
# signature, then the board colour.
#
# A name column is blank where that build's pack has no strip for the tsum,
# and the device then shows the other build's name, or the id. 0 rows
# carry only an English name, 84 only a Japanese one, and 38
# neither.
#
# Both games read this one file, and the device picks the name column by the
# package in front. The roster is the union of the two builds': 28 rows
# are tsums only the Japanese game has, cut from its own art; every other row
# is cut from the global build's copy, which the Japanese build's re-export of
# the same sprite still scores against at 0.98 and better.
#
# The board colour is what that tsum reads as when the play loop scans a board
# of them: HSV, one hex byte per channel, derived by `board_colors.py` from the
# plain `_l` sprite. `scanBoardQuick` uses it as the reference "Link MyTsum
# first" picks a colour cluster against, in place of a screenshot of the skill
# button. 0 rows carry no colour, and fall back to that screenshot.
#
# The signature is the button squashed into MyTsumPortrait's grid: four bits per
# channel, three channels per kept cell, in `MyTsumPoints` order, packed two to
# a byte and base64'd. A 16 x 16 grid, masked to an ellipse with the
# level badge cut off the bottom, is 148 cells -- 444 nibbles,
# 222 bytes, 296 characters.
#
# Beside the bundle rather than inside it, and read on first use rather than at
# load: this is two thirds of what `index.js` used to weigh, it changes only
# when the game adds a tsum, and `roundStats.ts` is the only thing that reads
# it -- which records rounds without a tsum name when it is missing.
gap-tsum-portraits v2 grid=16 cells=148 tsums=797 board=chroma1
